    {
      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-regular-conditional-laws-C005",
      "verdict": "partial",
      "gap": "Durrett's Theorem 4.1.17 (p. 215) asserts that regular conditional distributions exist when (S,S) is nice, i.e. (p. 53) there is a 1-1 map phi from S into R with phi and phi^{-1} measurable; no Borel image is required, although his proof's step 'B=(phi^{-1})^{-1}(A) in R' uses one. The entry proves existence only for a coding onto a Borel set E and calls this 'the source's standard Borel case', but the route never states the source's nice-space claim or settles its status without a Borel image. Also absent is the source's account just before the theorem (p. 214): r.c.d.'s do not always exist, the first example being due to Dieudonne (1948) (with Doob 1953 and Faden 1985 for later work), because each countable disjoint family satisfies additivity only almost surely and with enough such families the exceptional sets pile up. The route records only the bare nonexistence statement (conditioning-independence-and-information-w02b-p15-008.tex, lines 219-246). Supply these.",
