    {
      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-regular-conditional-laws-C011",
      "verdict": "partial",
      "gap": "Durrett presents the sphere calculation as Example 4.1.8, Borel's paradox (p. 210): taking longitude theta and latitude phi, or theta in [0,pi) and phi in (-pi,pi] so that theta names the great circle and phi the angle on it, one might expect both to be uniform; theta is but phi is not, and the paradox 'evaporates' once phi is seen to be independent of theta, so the conditional law is the unconditional one, which is not uniform because there is more land near the equator than near the North Pole. The entry proves the independence and the nonuniform density but omits the name Borel's paradox, the naive expectation it corrects and the source's resolution; the name occurs on the route only in an unrelated Bayes-factor remark and in the off-route w02c-p15-002 chapter. Supply them.",
